---
import ContentLayout from '../layouts/ContentLayout.astro';

const asideLinks = [
  { label: 'What counts as a correction', href: '#what-counts-as-a-correction' },
  { label: 'How to report an issue', href: '#how-to-report-an-issue' },
  { label: 'How reports are reviewed', href: '#how-reports-are-reviewed' },
  { label: 'Safety corrections', href: '#safety-corrections' },
  { label: 'What we cannot promise', href: '#what-we-cannot-promise' },
];
---

<ContentLayout
  title="Corrections policy"
  metaTitle="Corrections Policy: Reporting an Error"
  description="How to report a factual error, outdated step, broken link or safety problem in a FixProblemNow guide, and how corrections are reviewed and published."
  canonical="/corrections-policy/"
  eyebrow="Accuracy"
  intro="Guides can become wrong when a menu moves, a download changes, or a device behaves differently from the documentation. This policy explains how to report an error and what happens next."
  updated="2026-11-15"
  asideLinks={asideLinks}
  asideNote="For a safety issue, say so clearly in the message so it can be prioritised."
>
  <h2 id="what-counts-as-a-correction">What counts as a correction</h2>
  <ul class="checklist">
    <li>A factual statement that is wrong or unsupported by the linked source.</li>
    <li>A menu path, command, or setting that no longer exists in a current version.</li>
    <li>A step that is in the wrong order or can cause avoidable data loss.</li>
    <li>A broken link to official documentation.</li>
    <li>A missing warning about physical safety, battery swelling, liquid, or data erasure.</li>
    <li>A guide that applies to the wrong device, operating system, or category.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Differences of opinion about which fix is most likely are not corrections. If a guide works for
    most devices but not yours, that is useful context, but the guide may still be accurate for the
    documented case. We are interested in both kinds of report; they are handled differently.
  </p>

  <h2 id="how-to-report-an-issue">How to report an issue</h2>
  <p>
    Use the <a href="/contact/?topic=correction">contact form</a> and include:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>The full guide URL or title.</li>
    <li>The device model and operating-system version.</li>
    <li>The exact section, sentence, command, or step that is wrong.</li>
    <li>What you observed instead, with the exact error text if there is one.</li>
    <li>A link to official documentation that supports the correction, if you have one.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Do not send passwords, recovery keys, one-time codes, or personal files. A screenshot is helpful
    only if it does not expose private information.
  </p>

  <h2 id="how-reports-are-reviewed">How reports are reviewed</h2>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li>
      <strong>Check the report against the current guide.</strong>
      <p>
        We confirm which version of the guide the report refers to and whether the quoted text is
        still present.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Compare with official documentation.</strong>
      <p>
        The relevant manufacturer or operating-system source is checked. If the source has changed,
        the guide is updated. If the report cannot be reproduced or supported, the guide may remain
        unchanged.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Update the guide and its date.</strong>
      <p>
        Confirmed corrections change the article content and the visible update date. If the change
        is substantial, the affected section is rewritten rather than patched with a contradictory
        note.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Record the outcome.</strong>
      <p>
        Correction reports and their outcomes are kept long enough to avoid duplicate work and to
        spot repeated problems with a guide. Personal contact details are not published.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="safety-corrections">Safety corrections</h2>
  <p>
    A missing or incorrect safety warning is treated as a priority. If a guide could lead someone to
    charge a swollen battery, open a power supply, ignore liquid damage, or erase data without a
    backup warning, report it immediately and state “safety” in the message. The guide may be
    temporarily amended or unpublished while the issue is checked.
  </p>
  <div class="callout callout--important">
    <p class="callout__label">If you are in danger</p>
    <p>
      Do not wait for a website correction. Disconnect power if it is safe to do so, move away from
      smoke or a burning smell, and contact your local emergency service when appropriate. For a
      swollen battery, use a qualified repair or disposal service.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="what-we-cannot-promise">What we cannot promise</h2>
  <p>
    We cannot guarantee an individual reply time, a remote diagnosis, or a fix for your specific
    device. We also cannot mediate a warranty dispute or recover an account. What we can do is review
    a specific, evidence-based report and correct the published guidance when it is wrong.
  </p>
  <p>
    See the <a href="/editorial-policy/">editorial policy</a> for the standards used when a guide is
    written and the <a href="/how-it-works/">how it works page</a> for the risk-level definitions.
  </p>
</ContentLayout>
